> >> The MPR attempts to balance the issues of AC, XL, and weight management as
> >> you move into mid game.
>
> >No, it doesn't; it's way unbalanced toward having AC but no XL in the
> >early game. By the early mid-game, everyone should have full protection
> >(or close to it) whether they play the racket or not; the racket just
> >lets you have it throughout part of the early game.
>
> Again I disagree here. Getting protection later is much more costly in
> time and effort. Also you have to carry the extra weight in armor around
> fighting tougher monsters because of XL while you do it. Doing the MPR at
> low XLs unbalances the game in the players favor:

No it doesn't.  Getting protection earlier means you're wandering
around longer with low HP, making you highly susceptible to being
one-shotted by attack wands, squashed by rolling boulders, etc.  It's
more costly in terms of exposure to danger and lack of all kinds of
resources (Pw, skill slots, to-hit ability, etc).

> 1) Low XLs make it cheaper to get the protection. So you get it faster.

Yes, that's the whole point of the MPR.  Of course, you give up all the
other benefits of higher XLs during the run.  You're pushing for the
extreme of "AC at all costs", whereas a more balanced approach gets you
some of that AC but also limits your exposure with crappy HP, inability
to hit, low Pw, etc.

> 3) Getting protection earlier means that you can ditch the heavy armor
> required to keep up with the dungeon earlier.

This strikes me as an odd point of view.  Heavy armor isn't required to
keep up with the dungeon earlier--I almost never wear anything but
leather, studded leather, mithril, or DSM.  And if you consider those
"heavy", I'm pretty sure ditching them once you get protection isn't
conducive to survival.

I guess the one thing that could make sense in the context of the MPR
is having wizards and other spellcasters wear mithril pre-protection
since you've limited them to such miserable Pw that they're pretty well
crippled as casters.  But you actually get to ditch that armor earlier
if you don't do an MPR (indeed, you may never wear it to begin with).

> Leveling up generally only gives one benefit for a character that's not
> trying to kill monsters, and that's extra PPs

This is just absurd.  Extra hit points, for one, are a massive benefit.
 Lower spell failure (and learning) rates, skill slots, intrinsics, etc
can all be important as well.

> I've outlined how to gain HPs without leveling up

You can gain a few from prayer, but you can get those hp and many more
if you level up.  The more hp you can get, the less you have to fear
random HP-removing events--including the many that do not respect AC.

> >Sample size of one is pretty irrelevant, a lot of people can get to
> >75-80% success rate with the racket and it wouldn't be too surprising
> >to see you succeed with 4-5 in a row.
>
> But it does point to the fact that it isn't a low percentage play. If I
> played every character this way and only 1 in 5 got to minetown and got
> protection, I'd quickly abandon the strategy.

Right, but failing 1 in 5 times is enough to make it a low percentage
play compared to the options.  That's exactly why the people who can
ascend 10+ times in a row don't use it--it's adding an additional
chance of the game ending in death that's easily avoided.

> >The thing is that without it you can be much more successful; the key
> >to high percentage play in Nethack is getting rid of sheer random
> >chances for death and keeping you in the position where you have a
> >chance to think about every threat and deal with it.
>
> The problem is that leveling up increases the chances of dying.

It actually doesn't if done rationally, the benefits to the character
vastly outstrip the tougher monsters created.  There are a few tipping
points (getting MC or PR before seeing poisonous things is nice, though
they tend to respect E anyway) but in general it's a net win to level
up in a balanced manner.

> Each character acquires attributes that helps them deal with that. But when you
> get it out of balance, you often lose.

Exactly why an unbalanced approach like running to minetown at XL3 or
lower increases the odds of losing.  Balance is key.

> There are these types of decision points throughout the game. For example
> I never ever dip in fountains. I find the benefit is small vs. the potential
> risks. Of course I could use that early wish too.

Ahh, this is a good one.  Dipping for wishes is definitely worth the
benefit, but not when you're at low levels.  You need to be tough
enough to deal with all the potential consequences, but if you are then
the risk of dipping can be very minimal and the payoff of getting MR or
reflection can easily outweigh it.

If I'm XL10 with 90 HP, AC -10, and good attack capabilities (and have
some resources--e.g a wand of fire/lightning/teleport for emergencies,
poison resistance, etc), I'll certainly try to dip if I don't have MR
or reflection.  What's the worst that can happen?  If a water
demon/nymph/moccasin shows up, I can crunch it easily--the demon
_could_ gate in a big nasty, but the odds of that are quite low
compared to the odds of not having MR until after the Quest or Castle
doing me in.

At XL1 with no resources, the calculus is completely different--the bad
consequences are bordering on certain death, and dipping is just
foolish.

> >Even without the racket, my last (only?) death in rkrobin (where I was
> >doing all of the early game for a good while) was because of an early
> >attack wand that a gnome zapped me with before I had a chance to get
> >enough HP to turn such random occurances into something I can deal
> >with.
>
> I had one of thoses. A gnome with a wand of death on the 3rd level of Sokoban.
> It happens.

Yes, but the random attack wand isn't an instadeath if you have more
than a handful of HP.  Getting up to 50 HP is not that hard and
eliminates a lot of things that are practically instadeaths with lower
HP.

> >The traditional protection racket doesn't allow that, it keeps random
> >wands, rolling boulders, etc as kill possibilities instead of making
> >them something the player can deal with.
>
> So you believe that the extra HP acts as a buffer against that first surprise
> attack. I agree with you to a point. But I find that it comes at the cost of
> having a lot stronger normal attacks against that buffer.

As an experienced MPR addict, you should be able to deal with most of
those nasties comfortably without letting them beat you to death.
Those soldier ants, bees, etc all respect E just fine, thank you.
Generating somewhat tougher monsters doesn't eliminate all the ways you
have at your disposal to deal with them.

> >Eliminate instadeaths ruthlessly.  In the early game, every /fire,
> >/lightning, rolling boulder, etc is a potential instadeath.
>
> But they are relatively low risk events in the early game. And for each of
> these, that buffer only saves you from the first hit. Any monster with with
> what I call a $175 AGO (a good one) wand is likely to get two or three
> cracks at you.

Yes, so the more HP you can get the more turns you have to take it
down, run for a corner, dig to another level, or otherwise deal with
it.  It gives you a chance to apply strategy instead of just being at
the mercy of the not-so-merciful RNG.

Balancing that benefit of HP against other factors is necessary, but
ignoring it is just foolish.



